  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4t).$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
